Why This Is Asked
- Demonstrates modeling hierarchical command structures with a single interface.
- Shows uniform treatment of leaves and composites, enabling recursive execution.
- Tests understanding of registration and delegation without touching client logic.

Key Concepts
- Composite pattern
- Uniform interface for leaves/composites
- Recursive command resolution
- Lightweight plugin-friendly design

Code Example
```javascript
class Command {
  execute(args) { throw new Error('not implemented'); }
  help() { throw new Error('not implemented'); }
}
class LeafCommand extends Command {
  constructor(name, action){ super(); this.name = name; this.action = action; }
  execute(args){ this.action(args); }
  help(){ return this.name; }
}
class CommandGroup extends Command {
  constructor(name){ super(); this.name = name; this.children = []; }
  add(cmd){ this.children.push(cmd); }
  execute(args){ const [sub] = args; const found = this.children.find(c => c.help() === sub);
    if(found) found.execute(args.slice(1));
  }
  help(){ return this.name + ' (' + this.children.map(c => c.help()).join(', ') + ')'; }
}

// Usage
const root = new CommandGroup('root');
const status = new LeafCommand('status', () => console.log('ok'));
const repo = new CommandGroup('repo');
repo.add(status);
root.add(repo);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design pluggable, runtime-extendable components with clean isolation between tenants and core logic. It exercises registry design, immutable pipelines, and safe runtime extension.

## Key Concepts
- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry + factory pattern
- Runtime extensibility with per-tenant manifests
- Immutability and thread-safety in pipelines

## Code Example
```javascript
class ILogProcessor {
  async process(entry, next) { throw new Error('not implemented') }
}

class ProcessorRegistry {
  constructor(){ this.factories = new Map() }
  register(name, factory){ this.factories.set(name, factory) }
  buildPipeline(names){
    let next = async () => {}
    for (let i = names.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const F = this.factories.get(names[i])
      const proc = new F()
      const prev = next
      next = async () => { await proc.process(null, prev) }
    }
    return next
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle processor ordering changes at runtime while preserving ongoing log streams?
- How would you test pipelines for tenant isolation and regressions when new processors are added?

